---
import GuideLayout from "@/layouts/GuideLayout.astro";
import { GUIDES_BY_SLUG } from "@/data/guides";
const g = GUIDES_BY_SLUG["convert-minutes-to-decimal-hours"]!;
---

<GuideLayout title={g.title} metaTitle={g.metaTitle} description={g.description} slug={g.slug} updated={g.updated}>
  <p>
    Payroll systems multiply hours by a pay rate, so they need time as a
    <strong>decimal</strong> — 8.75 hours, not 8 hours 45 minutes. Converting is
    one division:
  </p>
  <p><code>decimal hours = whole hours + (minutes &divide; 60)</code></p>
  <p>
    8 hours 45 minutes is <code>8 + 45/60 = 8 + 0.75 = 8.75</code>. The reverse
    is <code>minutes = decimal part &times; 60</code>: the ".2" in 6.2 hours is
    <code>0.2 &times; 60 = 12</code> minutes.
  </p>

  <h2>The five you should memorise</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Minutes</th><th>Decimal</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>15 minutes</td><td>0.25</td></tr>
      <tr><td>20 minutes</td><td>0.33</td></tr>
      <tr><td>30 minutes</td><td>0.50</td></tr>
      <tr><td>40 minutes</td><td>0.67</td></tr>
      <tr><td>45 minutes</td><td>0.75</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    A common mistake is writing 8 hours 30 minutes as "8.30". The decimal for
    30 minutes is 0.50, so it is <strong>8.50</strong> hours. "8.30" would be
    18 minutes.
  </p>

  <h2>Full minute-to-decimal chart</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Min</th><th>Dec</th><th>Min</th><th>Dec</th><th>Min</th><th>Dec</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>1</td><td>0.02</td><td>21</td><td>0.35</td><td>41</td><td>0.68</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2</td><td>0.03</td><td>22</td><td>0.37</td><td>42</td><td>0.70</td></tr>
      <tr><td>3</td><td>0.05</td><td>23</td><td>0.38</td><td>43</td><td>0.72</td></tr>
      <tr><td>4</td><td>0.07</td><td>24</td><td>0.40</td><td>44</td><td>0.73</td></tr>
      <tr><td>5</td><td>0.08</td><td>25</td><td>0.42</td><td>45</td><td>0.75</td></tr>
      <tr><td>6</td><td>0.10</td><td>26</td><td>0.43</td><td>46</td><td>0.77</td></tr>
      <tr><td>7</td><td>0.12</td><td>27</td><td>0.45</td><td>47</td><td>0.78</td></tr>
      <tr><td>8</td><td>0.13</td><td>28</td><td>0.47</td><td>48</td><td>0.80</td></tr>
      <tr><td>9</td><td>0.15</td><td>29</td><td>0.48</td><td>49</td><td>0.82</td></tr>
      <tr><td>10</td><td>0.17</td><td>30</td><td>0.50</td><td>50</td><td>0.83</td></tr>
      <tr><td>11</td><td>0.18</td><td>31</td><td>0.52</td><td>51</td><td>0.85</td></tr>
      <tr><td>12</td><td>0.20</td><td>32</td><td>0.53</td><td>52</td><td>0.87</td></tr>
      <tr><td>13</td><td>0.22</td><td>33</td><td>0.55</td><td>53</td><td>0.88</td></tr>
      <tr><td>14</td><td>0.23</td><td>34</td><td>0.57</td><td>54</td><td>0.90</td></tr>
      <tr><td>15</td><td>0.25</td><td>35</td><td>0.58</td><td>55</td><td>0.92</td></tr>
      <tr><td>16</td><td>0.27</td><td>36</td><td>0.60</td><td>56</td><td>0.93</td></tr>
      <tr><td>17</td><td>0.28</td><td>37</td><td>0.62</td><td>57</td><td>0.95</td></tr>
      <tr><td>18</td><td>0.30</td><td>38</td><td>0.63</td><td>58</td><td>0.97</td></tr>
      <tr><td>19</td><td>0.32</td><td>39</td><td>0.65</td><td>59</td><td>0.98</td></tr>
      <tr><td>20</td><td>0.33</td><td>40</td><td>0.67</td><td>60</td><td>1.00</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Values are rounded to two decimals. Payroll systems keep more precision internally.</p>

  <h2>Rounding: keep the thirds exact</h2>
  <p>
    10, 20, 40, and 50 minutes are repeating decimals (0.1666…, 0.3333… and so
    on). If you round each shift's minutes to two decimals and then add them up,
    small errors accumulate. The reliable method:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>Add up <em>total minutes</em> for the day or week first.</li>
    <li>Divide the grand total by 60 once, at the end.</li>
    <li>Round only the final figure, to two decimals.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Example: three shifts of 7h 50m, 8h 10m, and 6h 20m. Minutes:
    470 + 490 + 380 = 1,340. <code>1340 &divide; 60 = 22.33</code> hours.
  </p>

  <h2>Decimal hours vs time-clock rounding</h2>
  <p>
    Converting to decimal is exact arithmetic. It is different from an employer
    <em>rounding your punch times</em> to the nearest 5 or 15 minutes before the
    conversion — that is a separate policy with its own rules, covered in
    <a href="/guides/time-clock-rounding-rules/">time clock rounding rules</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Convert instantly</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href="/calculators/decimal-hours-calculator/">Decimal Hours
    Calculator</a> converts in both directions and shows the chart. The
    <a href="/calculators/hours-worked-calculator/">Hours Worked Calculator</a>
    and <a href="/calculators/time-clock-calculator/">Time Clock Calculator</a>
    take clock times and give you a decimal total directly.
  </p>

